     1	# Chapter 7 — The Reading, Inward
     2	
     3	The city grew all day at the end of the road, and he let it, because a thing that is going to be looked at from inside tomorrow should be looked at from outside today, honestly, for as long as the road allows.
     4	
     5	He catalogued it by the wayposts. At the post that said nine miles to the Line it was a bluff with three lines drawn on it. At the post that said six it was walls, and the walls had gates, and the gates had the road going through them in a switchback that he could trace with a finger if he had wanted to lift one. At the post that said three the walls had a colour — not grey; he had thought grey from the rise, and it was not; it was the ochre a stone goes when a river has been at its foot for longer than anyone has counted and the sun has been on its face for the same — and the roofs inside the rings had a colour too, slate, and the smoke was a colour, and all of it stood up out of the plain on the far side of the river like a thing that had decided a long time ago how it wanted to be seen and had been seen that way since.
     6	
     7	He did not look at the top of it. He had looked once, from the rise, and had not looked since, and he found that not looking at a thing was a discipline like any other — like not declaring, like not touching — and that he was good at disciplines, and that this one had a cost he had not priced, which was that a thing you do not look at is a thing you are always aware of the location of.
     8	
     9	Six of them on the road, and no mule, because the mule had gone north that morning with a healer's roll on it, and Karis's boxes were on three backs — Brom's good shoulder, Lira's, his own — and the kit's case on Vastin's, who had not been asked and had picked it up at the fork-stone without a word. Lira ahead. Brom on the foot, which walked, as she had said it would. Karis with the flat under her arm and her eyes on the bluff, reading it as she read everything, for the strata. Seln not anywhere he could point to. And Vastin — who had walked at the back of the column for four days, where a man walks who has not been told where to walk — walking, since the fork-stone, beside him, because the road was wide and kept and there was nowhere else on it to be, and because, Cael thought, the man had made a decision about the back of the column somewhere on the rise that morning and had not announced it, which was a thing the man was learning.
    10	
    11	Lira came back down the road once, from ahead, and walked beside him for a post's length, and said nothing, and looked at the city, and he saw her look at the three walls as a thing with a top, and then look away from the top, deliberately, as he had, and understood that she had made the same discipline of it without being asked to and without a healer's term in a log, because Lira had a thing of her own about the tops of walls, and had had it since a training hall at sixteen, and was still carrying it at nineteen on a kept road with two arms. She went back up. She did not say anything, and that was all that she had come down to say.
    12	
    13	They did not talk for the first two posts. That was correct. A man who has filed you for four years and a man who has been filed do not have a conversation ready; they have a distance, and the distance has to be walked before anything else can be.
    14	
    15	---
    16	
    17	He ran the reading at the six-mile post, at the halt, with his back to the squared timber and the river below him and the city across it, because he was going to walk into a place that read bodies tomorrow and a man who is going to be read should read himself first, and because he had not done it since a marsh, and a healer had said stop, and he had a thing to work out about what she had meant.
    18	
    19	He worked it out first, before his hands moved, because the working-out was the part he owed her.
    20	
    21	*Stop touching it.* She had said it at a marsh's edge with her hands taken back, after he had told her the reading with his own hand on her wrist, and she had said it about the still place — the thing at the middle of him that the reading found and found and found and could do nothing with — because she had watched him go at it three times in eighteen days, two of them running, like a tongue at a tooth, and had said what a healer says to a patient who has found a thing in himself and cannot leave it alone. She had not said *do not read yourself.* The reading was his. She had taught it to him at her direction and had written the cost down in advance and it was the tenth thing he owned, and a man does not stop counting his own inventory because a healer told him not to pick at one item in it. He would read. He would read around. He would not touch the thing she had told him not to touch, and if the reading returned what it always returned he would write that down and not go back for a second look, and that was the whole of the distinction, and he had it now, and could begin.
    22	
    23	Both hands. One on the sternum and the other under his back, between him and the post, which was the form she had shown him and the form he had used at a marsh, and let the tenth thing do what it did.
    24	
    25	It did what it did. He had learned, in the autumn, that there was no better way to say it, and had stopped trying to find one.
    26	
    27	The current went out of his hands into him the way water goes into a channel it has been given, and he followed it, because following was the reading and there was no other part. Ten of them. He named them as he came to them, in the inventory's words, because the inventory's words were the only ones he had that did not explain anything.
    28	
    29	Wind, first, because Wind was always first: the burst's channel, wide and fast and shallow, the thing that had been loud in a cut two days ago and was quiet now, with the quiet of a road after a cart has gone over it. Pressure, beside it — the Bronze from Ardenmere's, narrow, deep, a channel cut for one thing. Iron, which was not a channel; Iron was the bed, the thing the other currents ran on, and it was quiet too, and had been quiet since a hall. Compression, which sat where Iron sat and pushed back. Ember, small and hot and exact, Karis's, that he had never once used and could feel the whole length of. Shadow, which cost from waking and was costing now, a thin draw that never stopped, the seal's whole logic in one steady leak. Storm, the suite's, Daeva's, wide and rough and asleep. Anchor, which did not flow so much as *hold*, a thing with a grip in it, and he passed over it carefully because Anchor was the one that had been used both ways in a year and still had the feel of both. Blade, Ephram's, sharp and short. And Tide, the tenth, which was not a current he could follow, because it was the following — the instrument, the hands where the form put them, the thing doing the reading — and he had learned in the autumn that you could not read the reader, and had stopped trying to, and let it be the one entry in the inventory with no description under it.
    30	
    31	He had not run it since the marsh, and the marsh had been the day after a hall, with two of the ten spent to the frame and the ribs saying so, and the reading then had been a reading of damage — of where the current pooled and broke and ran wrong, which was what a healer's instrument was for. This was not that. Nothing was broken. The ten ran as they ran when nothing had been asked of them for a day, which was easily, and the reading, which had been built to find the place where a body was wrong, went along ten things that were not wrong and found nothing to do, and he felt it find nothing to do, and felt, under that, the first edge of the thought that would arrive at the end: that an instrument built to find a fault reports *no fault* as its whole vocabulary, and that *no fault* is not the same as *nothing there*.
    32	
    33	Ten. All flowing, or holding, or asleep, or costing. All going somewhere.
    34	
    35	And at the middle of them, where the ten went round, the still place.
    36	
    37	He read around it. He had said he would and he did: the current went along the ten and came to the place where the ten bent, and he felt them bend — Wind going round it, Iron going round it, all ten going round a thing the way a river goes round a stone that the river has never once moved and has stopped trying to — and he did not follow the current in. He stopped at the bend. He held there, at the edge, with the ten going round under his attention and the thing they went round returning what it always returned, which was nothing: no flow, no change, no temperature, no edge that he could feel from outside except the edge the ten made by going round it. Not the Quiet. He had written that three times and he did not need to write it a fourth; the Quiet was outside a man and turned Paths off, and this was inside him and turned nothing off, and the ten went round it and worked. Not a fragment. A fragment flowed. Older than the log. Observation continues.
    38	
    39	He held at the edge while the river said one thing below the post, and then he took the hands off himself, and the current went back into them, and he was a man with his back against a post looking at a city.
    40	
