    42	
    43	That was the reading. It was the fourth time he had run it and it had returned the fourth same nothing, and he sat with the nothing and, for the first time, did not go back for a second look, and in the not-going-back he found the thing he had not found the other three times, because the other three times he had been too busy going back.
    44	
    45	The reading felt current.
    46	
    47	That was all of it. That was what Oryn had taught him on a table in the sun: *listening with my hands to how you're built* — but the listening was for flow, for the thing that moved through the architecture, for where it pooled and broke and ran wrong. It was an instrument for movement. It was the best instrument for movement he had ever seen, and it had been built by a Path whose whole name was *current*, and it could no more read a thing that did not move than a cord could measure a thing that had no length.
    48	
    49	He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*.
    50	
    51	He sat with that. The river ran below him. The city stood across it with its three walls and its gates and the thing on its crown that he was not looking at, and he sat against a post with his hands on his knees and understood, plainly, that four readings had told him exactly one true thing about the still place, which was that it was not a matter of flow, and that he had been reading that one true thing as *nothing* for six months because the instrument had no other word for it.
    52	
    53	He tested it, because a finding that was not tested was a guess wearing a finding's coat, and he had heard that said at a slab two days ago and had agreed with it. He put the hands back where the form put them, the one on the sternum and the other under his back against the post, and ran the reading a fifth time — not to the middle; to the ten — and asked it, in the only way it could be asked, what it could tell him about a thing that did not move, and it told him what it had told him four times: the ten went round; the going-round was real; the thing they went round was, to an instrument for current, a place where the current was not. A cord on a stone. He took the hands off. Five readings, one finding, and the finding was about the cord.
    54	
    55	You cannot measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement.
    56	
    57	He did not have another instrument. He had ten things that were not a Path and every one of them was a thing that moved, or held, or burned, or cut, and not one of them was for reading a thing that did none of those. He had a cord that read everyone but him and two brass edges that read a hair off in his hands and a healer on a north track since first light who had had her hands in him twice and been told the rest, and got a floor, and a man across a river who, Karis said, read how things were built.
    58	
    59	*How things are built.* Not how they flowed. Built.
    60	
    61	He noted the word. He did not do anything with it, because it was Karis's word for a man he had not met, and a word for a man is not an instrument; and because he could feel, at the edge of the noting, the shape of a thing he did not want to have arrived at yet — that he was going to walk into a city tomorrow and keep a promise about a station and hand the one question he had never let anyone hold to a stranger with a Path he had never seen, and that the reason he was going to do it was that he had just found out, against a post, that he could not answer the question himself, and never had been able to, and had spent six months mistaking the instrument's silence for the thing's.
    62	
    63	He was afraid of that. He wrote it down, later, in the plain hand, because it was true, and because a healer had said the log did something to him and he wanted to find out what it did with a sentence like that in it. But he did not write it down at the post. At the post he sat with it, with the city across the river, and did not catalogue it, and did not turn it into a count, and let it be what it was, which was a nineteen-year-old with ten things and a hole in the middle of them that he had been measuring with the wrong tool, on a road, a day from the first registry he had walked toward on purpose in his life.
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	Vastin had sat down.
    68	
    69	That was new. He had not sat in front of them since the cut, on his knee; he had stood on the stick at every halt and had let the standing say what it said, and now he had sat, on the road's edge, ten strides from Cael's post, with the stick across his knees and his back to nothing, and was looking at the city too, and Cael understood, after a while, that the man had sat so that the two of them would be sitting, and that it was the nearest thing to an invitation the man had in him, and that he had been at it for some time.
    70	
    71	Cael got up and went and sat on the road's edge a stride from him.
    72	
    73	They looked at the city.
    74	
    75	"I read your file for four years," Vastin said, eventually. He said it to the city. "I want to say that first, because I have never said it to you and it is the only fact between us that is entirely mine. I read it when it was two pages and I read it when it was a case. I read the report from the hearing and the report from the tournament and the report I wrote myself, at a table, when I declined to read another. I know the date you Kindled and the date you were expelled and the date you crossed the Line, and I know the name of the man who carried your satchel out of the residence, because it was in the file. I know more facts about you than anyone alive except possibly the girl with two arms, and I have understood none of them." He was quiet a moment. "I am telling you this so that you know what I am, sitting here. I am the instrument they used. I am the thing that read you and could not parse you, and I am sitting on a road with the thing I could not parse, and neither of us has a rank, and I find that I do not know how to sit."
    76	
    77	Cael looked at the city.
    78	
    79	"You were the instrument," he said. "I was the error."
    80	
    81	"Yes."
    82	
    83	"That's all of it. That's every room we were ever in. They built a thing to read people, and you were the part that read, and I was the part it couldn't." He turned it over once. "And now there's no thing. There's a road. There's you with a stick and me with a satchel and a cord that reads a hair wrong in my hands, and nobody on this road is going to read either of us, because the only instrument on it is across a river, and I've promised a healer I won't go near it."
    84	
    85	"I heard you promise."
    86	
    87	"Then you know what I'm walking into tomorrow with. Ten things and a promise."
    88	
    89	Vastin looked at him, then, for the first time since the fork — the count, and not a finding, because the man did not make findings any more; he had given that up somewhere between an office and a ferry and had not yet found the thing that went in its place. "I will tell you one thing I never put in the file," he said, "and then I have said everything I came up this road to say, and we can walk. I declined to read you a second time. You know that. What you do not know is why. I declined because I had read the first one twice, alone, at night, and had understood that if I read it a third time I was going to have to decide what it meant, and I was not — at fifty-one, with a career behind me — going to be the man who decided that. I sent it up. I let it become a designation. I let a boy carry my not-deciding across a continent as a piece of paper." He looked back at the city. "That is the thing I am. Not the instrument. The man who declined to be one, and called it procedure."
    90	
    91	Cael did not say anything for a while. He had carried the designation across a continent as a piece of paper and had known, in the way a man knows the weather, that somebody had signed it, and had not once, in four years, put a face to the signing, because a signature was a thing the Compact did and a face was a thing a man had, and the two had never been the same thing to him until a man on a road's edge had just made them the same thing on purpose. He did not forgive it. He had not been asked to. He noted that the man had not asked, and that the not-asking was the only part of it that a Compact officer would have got wrong, and that this one had got it right, and that getting it right had cost him something Cael could see and could not price.
    92	
    93	The river ran. Lira, up the road, was standing with her two hands at her sides looking at the walls, and Brom was sitting with the foot up, and Karis was reading the bluff, and Seln was somewhere.
    94	
    95	"I'll tell you one," he said. "Because you told me one and that's the only exchange rate I know." He looked at his own hands. "I've a thing in the middle of me that I've been reading for six months with an instrument that can't read it. I found that out just now, against that post. Every reading I've ever run on it came back nothing and I wrote *nothing* down four times, and it wasn't nothing; it was the tool. I've been putting a cord on a stone." He was quiet. "Nobody has that. Not Karis. Not the healer. Not the file. You're the first person I've said it to, and I've said it to you because you've no file to put it in."
    96	
    97	Vastin took that. Cael watched him take it — the stillness, the hands on the stick — and watched him not draft anything, which was a thing he had been taught at a spring and was keeping.
    98	
    99	"No," Vastin said. "I haven't."
   100	
   101	That was all. It was not friendship. He knew what friendship was; he had four of it and a fifth on a north road, and this was not that, and might never be. It was the ground the thing grows in, which is two men on a road's edge who have each told the other a fact that no institution holds, and have found that the road is still there under them afterward. He got up. Vastin got up, on the knee, with the stick, and did not need a hand, and they walked.
   102	
   103	---
   104	
   105	They camped short of the river, at the last post, with the city across the water close enough now to have a colour and the colour going out of it as the light went, and the light on its crown still there when the colour had gone.
   106	
   107	He did not look at it. He looked at the log.
   108	
   109	*Two hundred and eighty-first day. The last post short of the river. Six; the sixth on a north road. Brom's foot walks. A hundred and thirty-eight in hand.*
   110	
   111	*Ran the reading on myself again. Same nothing at the center. I think the reading can't do it — you can't measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement. I need an instrument for what doesn't move. Karis says there's a man in Treswick who reads how things are built. Maybe that's the instrument. Maybe I'm about to hand a stranger the one question I've never let anyone hold. Tomorrow. The walls are close enough now to have a color.*

