     1	# Chapter 8 — The Line at Treswick
     2	
     3	The bridge was older than the walls.
     4	
     5	He knew it before he was on it, because the walls were ochre and the bridge was grey, and grey was a colour he had learned to read in a year of floors: not the fused grey of a thing that had never had a join; the grey of stone that had been cut and laid and had stood in a river long enough for the river to give up on it. Five arches, at first light on the two hundred and eighty-second day, with the water high and brown under them and the road going over the crown of the middle one in a hump that a cart had to take slowly, and a toll-house at the far end with nobody in it, because the toll-house was for the road and the road's toll was taken at the gate.
     6	
     7	He catalogued the bridge because it was the last thing outside.
     8	
     9	Six of them on it, in the order they walked, with the boxes on their backs. Seln first, over the hump, and stopping on the far side to look at the gate as he looked at gates. Lira. Brom on the foot. Karis with the flat, reading the bluff above the bridge as it came closer and got taller and turned, at the last, from a thing you looked at into a thing you were under. Cael. And Vastin, at the back again, where he had walked into every town on the road, because a town was a place with a plank in it and the man had learned what a plank did to him.
