    14	
    15	The gate-keeper had not asked because the outer district was where paper was not asked for. That was its whole function. A city that ran on rank had to have a place at its foot where things that had no rank could be unloaded, and this was the place: the ring road, and the streets going off it into the space between the first wall and the second, and every one of them full of the things a rank does not have — carts, mules, bales, brokers' yards with tally-boards nailed up outside, a ferry landing on the river side with the season's first hides stacked on it, inns with two doors, a smith, a rope-walk, men and women moving between all of it with the walk of people who were paid by the load and not by the tier. The unranked district. The city's foot. He had lived at the foot of a city once, at fourteen, for a season, and had not known then that it was a mechanism; he had thought it was where he had ended up. It was not where anyone ended up. It was where the city put you, so that you could carry things for it without walking on its stairs.
    16	
    17	He looked up, then, once, because a man who is going to keep to a place should know the shape of what he is keeping out of.
    18	
    19	The second wall, above the roofs, with its gate in it — a smaller gate than the first, with a man in a coat at it that had a colour, and a queue of people going through it who each did a thing with their hand as they went that he could not see from here and could guess. A pass. The third wall above that, its gate smaller still, its queue shorter. And above the third wall the bluff's crown, and the roofs of the old town on it, and one roof taller than the rest with a square in front of it that he could not see and knew was there, and on the roof a thing he was not looking at.
    20	
    21	He was not looking at it. He looked at the ring road, and the smith, and the ferry landing, and a carter's mule at a trough by the gate.
    22	
    23	The mule did not care. He noted that with something that was not quite gratitude. A mule at the foot of a tiered city was exactly what it was at a ford or a marsh: a thing that carried, and was fed, and was not asked. The city had a column for the mule. It was the same column it had for him, and the mule had walked into it without braking for a step that was not there, and he found, watching the animal put its head down to a trough by the gate, that there was a lesson in that he did not want and was going to take anyway.
    24	
    25	---
    26	
    27	They took two rooms at an inn on the ring road that had a door on the street and a door on a yard, because Seln chose it, and Seln chose inns for their doors. Brom paid — two nights, in front, a mark a night for the six, which was the outer district's price for a room with a floor — and the woman who kept it counted six people and wrote six in a book and did not write what they were, because her book had no column for that, and Cael read her book across the counter, the wrong way round, out of a habit he had thought a winter would cure, and it had not.
    28	
    29	Then the crew did the thing it did in a new place, which was to go and look at it, each as each looked.
    30	
    31	He catalogued them going, because he was staying.
    32	
    33	Karis first, out the street door with the flat under her arm and the page on top, toward the second gate, because the registry office was on the old town and the scholars' registers she had read years ago said the provision was kept in a book at the registry and she wanted to see the outside of the building the book was in before she saw the inside of anything. She could not go through the second gate. She was unranked by choice, which was a thing the city priced exactly as it priced unranked by birth, and she knew it, and went to look at the gate anyway, because a gate was a stratum.
    34	
    35	Lira, out the yard door, toward the ring road's far side where the street went up toward the second wall in a stair — an actual stair, cut in the bluff, with a rail — because Lira had seen the stair from the bridge and had looked at it as she looked at a thing with a top. She could have gone up. Iron, first rank, formal, on paper from a circuit — the second tier was hers if she showed the paper, and she had the paper in her coat where she had carried it across a continent, and she did not take it out, and he watched her not take it out and go to look at the stair from its foot, with her two hands at her sides.
    36	
    37	Brom, nowhere, which meant the smith, because Brom priced a town by its smith; Copper, on paper, which at this gate bought him the outer district and a price for iron and nothing up a stair, and he had not asked for a stair.
    38	
    39	Seln, gone, as he went, which was not out of either door that anybody saw; a man with no standing a registry could write down and a name a Compact counter might, which made a registry counter the one place in a city he did not go, on principle and on a count.
    40	
    41	And Vastin, who had not gone anywhere. He sat in the inn's long room with his stick against his knee and his back to the wall, and when Cael sat down across from him he said, without being asked, "I have papers I will not show. I want that said once, so that nobody asks twice. They would open every gate on that hill and the station's door at the top of it, and I carried them out of an office because a man does not leave his own name behind on a desk, and I will not show them to a clerk in a city on the Line to save myself a stair." He looked at the stick. "I am in the district where the unranked are allowed, and it is the first honest district I have stood in."
    42	
    43	Cael did not say anything to that. There was not anything to say to it that the man had not already said, and he had said it once, and once was the number.
    44	
    45	Brom came back from the smith with the foot re-bound and a price for iron in his head and said, "They shoe by the tier," which was the whole of Brom's report on Treswick and was, from Brom, an essay: the smith at the city's foot shod carters' mules for coppers and had a second forge, up a stair, that shod ranked practitioners' horses for silver, and the same man worked both, and walked between them by a pass — a stamped tin on a cord, Brom said, shown at the stair's foot and not taken off. "Same hands," Brom said. "Different price. He doesn't like it either." He sat down with the foot up and did not say anything else, because he had said the thing.
    46	
