    52	
    53	He had spent nine months in a country that did not read him. It had not asked. It had not measured. It had priced what he did and written it in a book and stopped numbering him when the number got wrong, and he had written *no file I can see* in a log until the sentence had gone soft with use, and had found, in the winter, that a country that did not read you was also a country that did not answer, and had not liked finding it. And here was a country that read. Here was the whole machine — the tiers, the gates, the passes, the office on the old town, the thing on the crown — stacked up a hill in front of him in three rings with the sun on it, and it read everyone who walked up its stairs, and it would read him the moment he set a foot on one, and he stood at the foot of it with his hands at his sides and felt the walls as walls, which was a thing he had not felt since a bluff above Ostrand, and found that he had missed it, and did not know what to do with having missed it, and wrote that down.
    54	
    55	The road up to the registry went through the square.
