    58	
    59	He could not have gone up the stair. But if he could have, that was the road, and it went through the square, and a healer four days north had said *not the square*, and he had written it, and read it back, and here was the first place on the continent where the promise and the road were the same thing, and the promise held, because he stood at the foot of a stair he was not allowed on and did not try the other way round the bluff, which a man who wanted the registry badly enough might have tried, and which he catalogued himself not trying, and let stand.
    60	
    61	He went back along the ring road to the inn, by the smith, where Brom was, past a broker's yard where a man with a tally-board was counting bales by the load and not by anything else, and sat in the yard, and waited, because the district where the unranked were allowed was the district where the unranked waited, and he was going to find out what waiting was worth.
