    90	
    91	Lira had come in during the last of it, by the yard door, and had stood at the wall and listened, and when Seln had finished she said, to nobody, "Four hands," and then, "I've two," and looked at the left one, and Cael understood that she was pricing herself as labour, on purpose, in a city that would have taken her paper and put her on the second tier by noon, and had not yet decided which of the two things she was going to be here.
    92	
    93	He wrote it down. He wrote: *Entered by count. That's the door.* And under it, because the log did something to him and he wanted to find out what: *It's the right price. It's what I'm worth here. I'll carry the boxes.*
    94	
    95	---
    96	
    97	Karis went up in the morning, because the provision was worked in the morning when the registry opened, and because a thing that has been waited five years for is not waited a day longer than it has to be.
    98	
    99	She went out the street door with the flat under her arm and the page on top and the notebook in her hand and nothing else, because a working researcher attaching to a registered practitioner carries her work and not her boxes, and she stopped at the door and looked back at the long room, at the four of them, and at Cael, who was sitting at the table with the log open and the pencil in his hand and his back to the wall, in the district where the unranked were allowed.
   100	
   101	He got up and walked her to the ring road, because it was fifty paces and he had walked fifty paces beside people at the edge of things before.
   102	
   103	They stood at the foot of the stair. The stair went up the bluff to the second gate, and above the second gate the second wall, and above that the third, and above the third the crown of the hill with the old town on it and the registry's roof, and above the registry's roof the one taller roof and the thing on it with its light, and he did not look at the light. He looked at the stair, which he could not climb, and at Karis, who could not either, and who was going to stand at its foot and wait for a man in his sixties to come down it at noon to eat, and attach to him, and go up.
   104	
   105	"Last time we walked into a place like this," Cael said, "it took four years and a hearing to walk out."
   106	
   107	"Last time we didn't know what we were looking for."
   108	
   109	"Do we now?"
   110	
   111	Karis looked up the bluff.
   112	
   113	"A name and a reason," she said. "Older than the walls, under our feet, and nobody's read it because nobody's had a reason to look and a person who could read it in the same lifetime." A beat. "That's us. That's the whole reason it's still buried. We're the first ones who are both."
   114	
   115	She went to find the clerk.
   116	
   117	He stayed in the district where the unranked were allowed, at the foot of a stair, with a satchel on his shoulder and ten things in him and a promise in a book, and waited.

