     1	# Chapter 9 — Inside Walls
     2	
     3	Cleon came down the stair at noon on the two hundred and eighty-third day, because Seln had said he would, and Seln did not say a thing about a man's habits unless he had watched the habit twice.
     4	
     5	He came down it alone, with a book under his arm and his eyes on the steps, in the walk of a man who has taken the same stair to the same table for thirty years and has stopped seeing it: sixties, grey, a coat worn past noticing, and old ink in the creases of the right hand, which Cael, at the stair's foot with the log open, catalogued before the face, because the hands were the thing.
     6	
     7	He did not look at any of them. He came off the last step and turned along the ring road toward the tavern that Seln had said was his, on the third tier's landing, where the stair went up again, and Karis, who had been standing at the stair's foot with the flat under her arm for an hour, fell in beside him and said, "Cleon," and he said, "Yes," without slowing, and she said, "I have three sets of carvings off fused-stone floors that nobody on this side of the Line can read, and a word from a stratum older than the standardization, and I've been carrying both for a year," and he stopped.
     8	
     9	He stopped like a man who has been asked a question he has been waiting thirty years to be asked and has stopped expecting: not all at once; a step late, with the foot still going, and then the whole of him arriving at the stop after the foot. He did not turn round. He looked at the tavern's door ahead of him for a while.
