   146	
   147	He laid his right palm on his breastbone. He put the first two fingers of his left hand on the inside of his right wrist, where the healer put hers; and he let his breath go long, as a sleeper's does, and counted.
   148	
   149	At ten the short reading gave him the moving. It was his own gait, quick and slow, and a good deal quicker than it ought to have been in a person who was lying down.
   150	
   151	He went on beyond ten, into the deep reading. They came up out of it one at a time, as they always had, like the furniture of a room when the eyes have grown used to the dark.
   152	
   153	Wind was first, since it was the oldest, and it had always leaned toward a girl. It was a dry hiss in his feet, like pale grass going over in a breath of air. It was good for ten strides, and it had to be paid for at the end of them with a locked beat in which he could not have lifted a heel. He had spent it two days ago in a cut, running away from the people whom he was guarding.
   154	
