     3	The carters were gone before the two hundred and eighty-second day had any light in it, and they had left their fire raked out and a stack of thorn beside the ring for whoever came next.
     4	
     5	The six did the last three miles through river meadows, in a white mist that lay as high as Brom's belt. The road ran on a causeway there, with a ditch on either hand, and the town stood up out of the mist ahead of them like a thing set on a table. Cael kept his eyes on the foot of it. He had done so for a day now, and it had begun to be a habit of his neck.
     6	
     7	Then the causeway lifted, and they were at the river.
